Let's analyze the problem:

- We are given three characters: a, b, c, in some order.
- We can perform **at most one swap** between any two cards.
- The goal is to check whether, after zero or one swap, the row becomes "abc".

**Key points:**
- If the string is already "abc", the answer is "YES".
- If not, check if swapping any two characters results in "abc".
- If yes, answer "YES"; otherwise, "NO".

---

### Approach:
1. For each test case:
   - Check if the string is already "abc".
   - Otherwise, generate all possible strings obtained by swapping any two characters.
   - If "abc" is among these, output "YES"; otherwise, "NO".

### Implementation:
Here's the C++ code to solve this:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int t;
    cin >> t;
    while (t--) {
        string s;
        cin >> s;

        // Check if string is already "abc"
        if (s == "abc") {
            cout << "YES" << endl;
            continue;
        }

        bool canBe = false;

        // Try swapping each pair of positions
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < 3; ++j) {
                string temp = s;
                swap(temp[i], temp[j]);
                if (temp == "abc") {
                    canBe = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (canBe) break;
        }

        if (canBe)
            cout << "YES" << endl;
        else
            cout << "NO" << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

---

### Explanation:
- We read the number of test cases.
- For each string:
  - Check if it is already "abc".
  - Otherwise, try all swaps of two characters.
  - If any swap results in "abc", print "YES"; otherwise, "NO".

This approach ensures we cover all possible cases with minimal computation, suitable since the string length is fixed at 3.

Adding a toolbar within a Fragment in Android involves a few key steps. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you set up a toolbar inside a Fragment:

### 1. Define the Toolbar in Fragment Layout
First, include a `Toolbar` widget in your Fragment's layout XML file.

```xml
<!-- res/layout/fragment_example.xml -->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:orientation="vertical">

    <androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar
        android:id="@+id/toolbar"
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="?attr/actionBarSize"
        android:background="?attr/colorPrimary"
        android:theme="?attr/actionBarTheme" />

    <!-- Other UI components of your fragment -->
    <TextView
        android:layout_width="wrap_content"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:text="Fragment Content" />

</LinearLayout>
```

### 2. Set Up the Toolbar in the Fragment Class
In your Fragment class, initialize the toolbar and set it as the support action bar if needed.

```java
// ExampleFragment.java
import android.os.Bundle;
import android.view.LayoutInflater;
import android.view.View;
import android.view.ViewGroup;
import androidx.annotation.NonNull;
import androidx.annotation.Nullable;
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity;
import androidx.appcompat.widget.Toolbar;
import androidx.fragment.app.Fragment;

public class ExampleFragment extends Fragment {

    @Nullable
    @Override
    public View onCreateView(@NonNull LayoutInflater inflater, 
                             @Nullable ViewGroup container,
                             @Nullable Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        View view = inflater.inflate(R.layout.fragment_example, container, false);

        // Find the toolbar in the layout
        Toolbar toolbar = view.findViewById(R.id.toolbar);

        // Set up the toolbar as the ActionBar (if activity is AppCompatActivity)
        // Note: Make sure the hosting activity is an AppCompatActivity
        if (getActivity() instanceof AppCompatActivity) {
            AppCompatActivity activity = (AppCompatActivity) getActivity();
            activity.setSupportActionBar(toolbar);

            // Optional: Set the title
            activity.getSupportActionBar().setTitle("Fragment Toolbar");

            // Optional: Add menu items if needed
            setHasOptionsMenu(true);
        }

        return view;
    }

    // If you want to handle menu items in the toolbar
    @Override
    public void onCreateOptionsMenu(@NonNull android.view.Menu menu, @NonNull android.view.MenuInflater inflater) {
        super.onCreateOptionsMenu(menu, inflater);
        inflater.inflate(R.menu.fragment_menu, menu);
    }
}
```

### 3. (Optional) Add Menu Items
Create a menu resource file if you want to add menu items to the toolbar.

```xml
<!-- res/menu/fragment_menu.xml -->
<menu xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item
        android:id="@+id/action_settings"
        android:title="Settings"
        android:orderInCategory="100"
        android:showAsAction="never" />
</menu>
```

### 4. Handle Menu Item Selection
Override `onOptionsItemSelected()` in your Fragment if you want to handle menu actions.

```java
@Override
public boolean onOptionsItemSelected(@NonNull android.view.MenuItem item) {
    if (item.getItemId() == R.id.action_settings) {
        // Handle menu item
        return true;
    }
    return super.onOptionsItemSelected(item);
}
```

### Important Notes:
- The fragment's layout should include the `Toolbar`.
- The hosting activity should be an `AppCompatActivity` to support `setSupportActionBar()`.
- Call `setHasOptionsMenu(true)` in the fragment to enable menu handling.

---

**Summary:**  
To add a toolbar in a Fragment, include a `Toolbar` widget in the layout, initialize it in `onCreateView()`, set it as the support action bar in the activity, and optionally handle menu items.

