Certainly! To revise your function so that `Player1` is always the player with the lowest `Matches`, and `Player2` is chosen randomly from the remaining players each time, you can do the following:

1. Find the player with the minimum `Matches` — this will be `Player1`.
2. Create a list of remaining players (excluding `Player1`).
3. Randomly select `Player2` from that list.

Here's a revised version of your function:

```javascript
function elo(data) {
  if (data.length < 2) {
    // Not enough players to select two
    return;
  }

  // Find the player with the minimum Matches
  let Player1 = data.reduce((minPlayer, currentPlayer) => {
    return currentPlayer.Matches < minPlayer.Matches ? currentPlayer : minPlayer;
  }, data[0]);

  // Create a list of remaining players excluding Player1
  const remainingPlayers = data.filter(player => player !== Player1);

  // Randomly select Player2 from remainingPlayers
