To directly download the content of a webpage using the browser console, you can create a temporary link (`a` element) and trigger a click on it. Here's a simple example:

```js
// Replace 'YOUR_URL_HERE' with the URL of the page you want to download
const url = 'YOUR_URL_HERE';

// Fetch the content of the URL
fetch(url)
  .then(response => response.text())
  .then(data => {
    // Create a blob with the fetched data
    const blob = new Blob([data], { type: 'text/html' });
    // Create a temporary download link
    const link = document.createElement('a');
    link.href = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
    // Set the download attribute with a filename
    link.download = 'downloaded_page.html';
    document.body.appendChild(link);
    // Trigger the download
    link.click();
    // Clean up
    document.body.removeChild(link);
  })
  .catch(error => console.error('Error fetching the page:', error));
```

**Note:**
- Due to CORS policies, this will only work if the URL is on the same origin as your current page or if the server allows cross-origin requests.
- For cross-origin URLs that don't support CORS, browsers will block the fetch request.

**Alternative for same-origin URLs:**

If the page you want to download is on the same domain, you can simply do:

```js
window.location.href = 'YOUR_URL_HERE';
```

which will navigate to the page, or:

```js
window.open('YOUR_URL_HERE', '_blank');
```

to open it in a new tab. But to save directly as a file, the fetch method described above is suitable.

Certainly! Here's a Rust program that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from other URLs, and uses `yt-dlp` to download the YouTube URLs. This example assumes you have `yt-dlp` installed and accessible in your system's PATH.

### Prerequisites:
- Rust installed (https://rustup.rs/)
- `yt-dlp` installed (`pip install yt-dlp` or other method)
- A text file (`urls.txt`) containing URLs, one per line

---

### Rust Program:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader};
use std::process::Command;
use regex::Regex;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    // Path to the file containing URLs
    let file_path = "urls.txt";

    // Open the file
    let file = File::open(file_path)?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    // Regex to identify YouTube URLs
    let youtube_regex = Regex::new(r#"^(https?://)?(www\.)?(youtube\.com|youtu\.be)/"#).unwrap();

    // Collect URLs
    let mut youtube_urls = Vec::new();
    let mut other_urls = Vec::new();

    for line in reader.lines() {
        let url = line?;
        if youtube_regex.is_match(&url) {
            youtube_urls.push(url);
        } else {
            other_urls.push(url);
        }
    }

    println!("Found {} YouTube URLs.", youtube_urls.len());
    println!("Found {} other URLs.", other_urls.len());

    // Download YouTube URLs using yt-dlp
    for url in youtube_urls {
        println!("Downloading: {}", url);
        let status = Command::new("yt-dlp")
            .arg(&url)
            .status()
            .expect("Failed to execute yt-dlp");
        if status.success() {
            println!("Successfully downloaded: {}", url);
        } else {
            eprintln!("Failed to download: {}", url);
        }
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

