Since XAML alone isn't well-suited for complex recursive generation, we'll define a simple static example. For dynamic, recursive generation, you'd typically use code-behind (C#), but I'll provide a pure XAML example for a static Sierpinski Triangle.

### Static Sierpinski Triangle in XAML

```xml
<Window x:Class="SierpinskiTriangle.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Sierpinski Triangle" Height="600" Width="600">
    <Canvas Background="White">
        <!-- Largest Triangle -->
        <Polygon Points="300,50 50,550 550,550" Fill="Black"/>
        <!-- Top middle small triangle -->
        <Polygon Points="300,50 175,300 425,300" Fill="White"/>
        <!-- Bottom left small triangle -->
        <Polygon Points="50,550 175,300 50,300" Fill="White"/>
        <!-- Bottom right small triangle -->
        <Polygon Points="425,300 550,550 300,550" Fill="White"/>
        <!-- Further smaller triangles can be added for more detail -->
    </Canvas>
</Window>
```

### Explanation:
- The main large triangle is drawn with `Polygon`.
- Smaller white triangles cut into the black triangle to form the Sierpinski pattern.
- This is a static representation; for more levels of recursion, you'd need to generate these shapes dynamically using code-behind.

---

### Dynamic Recursive Sierpinski Triangle (C# code-behind idea)
If you'd like a recursive approach, you'd typically generate the triangles in code (C#), then add them to the Canvas. Here's a conceptual snippet:

```csharp
// Example method to recursively add triangles
void DrawSierpinski(Canvas canvas, Point p1, Point p2, Point p3, int level)
{
    if (level == 0)
    {
        var triangle = new Polygon
        {
            Points = new PointCollection { p1, p2, p3 },
            Fill = Brushes.Black
        };
        canvas.Children.Add(triangle);
    }
    else
    {
        // Calculate midpoints
        Point m12 = MidPoint(p1, p2);
        Point m23 = MidPoint(p2, p3);
        Point m31 = MidPoint(p3, p1);

        // Recursively draw smaller triangles
        DrawSierpinski(canvas, p1, m12, m31, level - 1);
        DrawSierpinski(canvas, m12, p2, m23, level - 1);
        DrawSierpinski(canvas, m31, m23, p3, level - 1);
        // Optional: fill the central inverted triangle if desired
    }
}

Point MidPoint(Point p1, Point p2)
{
    return new Point((p1.X + p2.X) / 2, (p1.Y + p2.Y) / 2);
}
```

You can invoke this method in your code-behind (e.g., in the `Loaded` event) to generate the full Sierpinski Triangle dynamically.

