Certainly! Here's a simple example of a C# program that sends a POST request with a client certificate attached. This example uses `HttpClient` along with `HttpClientHandler` to configure the client certificate.

Make sure you have the client certificate available as a `.pfx` file and know its password.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace PostRequestWithClientCertificate
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            // Path to your client certificate (.pfx file)
            string certPath = "path/to/your/client_certificate.pfx";
            // Password for the certificate
            string certPassword = "your_cert_password";

            // URL to send the POST request to
            string url = "https://yourapiendpoint.com/api/resource";

            // Data to send in the POST request
            string postData = "{\"key1\":\"value1\", \"key2\":\"value2\"}";
            var content = new StringContent(postData, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

            // Load the client certificate
            var clientCertificate = new X509Certificate2(certPath, certPassword);

            // Create handler and attach the client certificate
            var handler = new HttpClientHandler();
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(clientCertificate);

            // Create HttpClient with the handler
            using (var httpClient = new HttpClient(handler))
            {
                try
                {
                    // Send POST request
                    HttpResponseMessage response = await httpClient.PostAsync(url, content);

                    // Read response
                    string responseContent = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();

                    Console.WriteLine($"Status Code: {response.StatusCode}");
                    Console.WriteLine("Response:");
                    Console.WriteLine(responseContent);
                }
                catch (Exception ex)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("An error occurred:");
                    Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Important notes:
- Replace `"path/to/your/client_certificate.pfx"` with the actual path to your certificate file.
- Replace `"your_cert_password"` with the password for your certificate.
- Replace `"https://yourapiendpoint.com/api/resource"` with your actual endpoint URL.
- Adjust the `postData` string as needed for your request.

### Additional considerations:
- Make sure your certificate has the necessary private key.
- If your certificate is stored in the certificate store, you can load it differently (e.g., using `X509Store`).
- For production code, consider handling exceptions and disposing resources properly.

